# Line endings and diff behaviour, pinned in the repository rather than left to
# each clone's `core.autocrlf`. Git for Windows defaults that to `true`, which
# would rewrite checked-out files — and some files here must survive byte for
# byte. See the fixtures and vendor sections below.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- default
#
# Normalise to LF in the repository and in the working tree. Everything in this
# tree is already LF, so this pins the status quo rather than causing a
# renormalisation.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------- signed test fixtures
#
# TUF signatures cover canonical JSON bytes. An EOL rewrite on checkout would
# invalidate every signature in these files, and the resulting interop failures
# look nothing like a line-ending problem. `-text` disables conversion in both
# directions while keeping the files readable in a diff.
**/tests/fixtures/**            -text
**/tests/fixtures/**/*.bin      binary

# --------------------------------------------------------- vendored tuf fork
#
# Kept byte-identical to the crates.io package so the diff against upstream
# shows only the six changes in PATCHES.md and nothing else.
client/vendor/**                -text linguist-vendored
client/vendor/tuf/tests/**      binary

# ------------------------------------------------------------ binary content
#
# Key material and archives. Never converted, never diffed as text — a textual
# diff of a DER key is noise at best and a partial key disclosure in a review
# comment at worst.
*.der       binary
*.pk8       binary
*.spki      binary
*.kdbx      binary
*.keyx      binary
*.p12       binary
*.pfx       binary
*.jks       binary
*.bin       binary
*.exe       binary
*.dll       binary
*.so        binary
*.dylib     binary
*.pdb       binary
*.zip       binary
*.gz        binary
*.tar       binary
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.ico       binary
*.pdf       binary

# ------------------------------------------------------- runs inside a container
#
# These are consumed by Linux processes. A CRLF shebang is an exec failure with
# an error message that names the wrong thing, so state the requirement rather
# than relying on the default above staying put.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.conf          text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
Dockerfile.*    text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
.env.example    text eol=lf

# ----------------------------------------------------------- windows tooling
#
# The client is Windows-first (PLAN.md 6). Batch files genuinely require CRLF.
# Note this normalises to LF in the repository and restores CRLF on checkout,
# so it is byte-stable only for a file that was CRLF to begin with. An
# Authenticode-signed script must therefore never be committed here — put it
# under a tests/fixtures path, which is `-text` and preserved exactly.
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.ps1       text eol=crlf
*.psm1      text eol=crlf
*.sln       text eol=crlf
*.csproj    text eol=crlf

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ diagrams
#
# mxGraph XML. A three-way merge of a diagram produces plausible-looking
# corruption, so require a human to choose a side instead.
*.drawio    text eol=lf merge=binary

# -------------------------------------------------------------- diff drivers
#
# Better hunk headers: git names the enclosing function or section.
*.py        diff=python
*.rs        diff=rust
*.md        diff=markdown
*.h         diff=cpp
*.c         diff=cpp
*.cpp       diff=cpp
*.cs        diff=csharp

# Deliberately NOT marked linguist-generated: Cargo.lock and uv.lock are the
# dependency review surface for supply-chain changes (PLAN.md 4.1), and
# collapsing them by default in review is the opposite of what we want.
